  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Saturday...\n\nSatellite imagery shows dry/flat ridging over the region. Surface\nanalysis shows 850mb cold advection associated with a weak front\nnosing from north central MT to the NW portion of our cwa, and\njust recently we are seeing NW winds increase from Wheatland to\nMusselshell Counties. A Red Flag Warning is in effect thru 9pm \nthis evening for a combination of hot (90s to near 100F), dry \n(7-14%) and breezy (20-30 mph gusts) conditions. Please be careful\nnot to spark a fire. Current air quality is \"unhealthy\" from \nLivingston to Billings and down toward Sheridan, with slightly \nbetter conditions to the north and east. Expect modest improvement\ntoday as NW-N winds increase. Generally speaking, the westerly \nflow aloft will continue to advect ample smoke from the numerous \nwildfires over the PacNW.\n\nThere is a slight increase in monsoon moisture over southern ID \nthat is lifting toward south central MT today. Also, an upstream \nshortwave along the WA coast is moving eastward. The combination \nof these upstream features will yield some late day cumulus over \nthe Beartooth-Absarokas (perhaps virga?) and some increase in \nmid/high clouds from west to east tonight into Sunday morning. \nForecast soundings show some very elevated moisture and \ninstability (near 500mb) and this should yield areas of high-based\nACCAS and perhaps virga. Very dry boundary layer remains so will \nnot mention any risk of showers. Otherwise, Sunday will be not- \nas-warm, not-as-dry and not-as-windy as today. Fire weather \nconditions remain elevated through tomorrow. By tomorrow evening, \nas southeasterly low level jet develops out of western SD, we \ncould see a few weak t-storms pop up in far southeast MT. This is \nuncertain, but if it happens would be a sign of the air mass \nstarting to moisten.\n\nMonday & Tuesday will remain warm (upper 80s to mid 90s) but pwats\nwill gradually increase, per high plains advection and to a lesser\nextent high level monsoonal flow. Any chance of convection on \nthese days will be low (10-20%) and confined to areas near the \nDakotas border. The west half of our cwa will be much drier and \nsee min RHs continuing to drop to the low-mid teens. Lightning in \nour far east could trigger fire starts given the dry fuels.\n\nEnsembles in good agreement showing pwats continuing to increase,\nand values approaching 1\" as far west as Billings by Wednesday.\nForcing is a bit uncertain but it would appear that Wednesday is\nthe first day when t-storm potential increases further west to at\nleast include central portions of our cwa. This also drops\nforecast highs to the 80s.\n\nExpect a greater potential for showers and t-storms along with\ncooler temps Thursday through Saturday, in response to elevated\npwats and a stronger trof dropping out of southwest Canada. None\nof this looks particularly dynamic and temps won't be cold (highs\nin 70s to lower 80s), but the potential for actual precipitation \nis much higher (20-50% chance each of these 3 days). Humidities \nwill be seasonably high to end the week (mins above 40%) but the \nincreasing risk of lightning could be problematic given the hot & \ndry weather happening now. Most of our cwa has not seen measurable\nprecip yet this month, and won't over the next 4 days.\n\nJKL",
